    "q-22568": {
      "dom_id": "step-q22568",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "For the function $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x},$ defined on the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},2],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The global maximum value is $\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}$\n\nII. The global minimum value is $2\\sqrt{e}$\n\nIII. The global minimum value is $4\\sqrt[4]{e}$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot x-{e}^{x}\\cdot 1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(x-1)}{{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}(x-1)}{{x}^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=1,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{{e}^{1/2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2\\sqrt{e}≈3.3 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{1}=e≈2.7 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}≈3.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(2)=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=e,$ so the global minimum is $e.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14221929/assets/98156f05b9b6c71c7e5a5753d024d0851a0a779741545ba2d62d3d63af41e824.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
