    "q-2689": {
      "dom_id": "step-q2689",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-8{x}^{2}$ on the closed interval $-3\\le x\\le 1.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{4}-8{x}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-16x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-16x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ 4x(x-2)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ does not belong to the interval $[-3,1]$, the critical points are $x=-2,0,$ and the endpoints $x=-3,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =(-3)^{4}-8(-3)^{2}=9 \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2)^{4}-8(-2)^{2}=-16 \\\\ f(0) & =(0)^{4}-8(0)^{2}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =(1)^{4}-8(1)^{2}=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-3)=9,$ so the global maximum is $9.$\n\n- The least value is $f(-2)=-16,$ so the global minimum is $-16.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14221929/assets/e57ff9bc04fb9d9db592dda6aac9c364fd5f2e6fcd52968627b1e0271919f5bb.png)",
